{"kind":"request","text":"C3 precode question from inventory f8f35048: confirm the failure behavior of admit-before-build for an EXISTING identity.\n\nProposed patch wraps both eligible #resigned paths in the existing store.exclusive section, runs #admitted only for a refused record, then calls store.admit(id, bytes + RECORD_OVERHEAD) before build. That reuses #kept's forecast convention; SQLite already ignores this forecast and computes the whole remaining completion need from its stored record rows. Count/budget refusal happens before build, signing or record write; no new budget/port/protocol.\n\nIf admit succeeds but build/signing then fails, #kept's new-identity cleanup cannot safely be copied. CustodyStore.unadmit is documented for an identity whose preparation produced no record. SQLite unadmit releases only when no live record row exists; it cannot restore just an existing identity's added reservation, and another implementation may release a reservation the earlier unresolved record still needs.\n\nI propose no unadmit on this existing-identity path: preserve earlier raw record bytes and any existing completion reservation. A successful top-up can then remain after build failure, including on a refused record until a later result/terminal write releases it. This conservatively holds capacity; it does not silently release an earlier copy's completion need. Confirm this behavior, or give another bounded disposition without inferring a new cleanup protocol. Source remains unchanged; the original C3 promise a5598874 stays active, and all execution/integration waits for root's slot/prerequisites.","body":{"conditions":"Confirm the existing-identity admission failure behavior before C3 source: preserve any successful completion top-up across a later build failure, with no unadmit call or new cleanup protocol, or state another bounded disposition.","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:39e6eeadbda8eb3c7a